/// ============================ TRIPWIRE ==================================
///
/// THE FIRST TEST BELOW PINS BEHAVIOR docs/name-resolution.md SAYS IS WRONG.
/// It passes today because the compiler is wrong today.
///
/// IF YOU ARE HERE BECAUSE IT STARTED FAILING: that is the intended outcome,
/// and it means someone landed the visibility gate. Do NOT delete the
/// assertion to get back to green. Convert this project to `compile_fail`
/// with `expect.diagnostic` E0353, in the same change that made it an error.
///
/// Same trust-loop reasoning as tests/tests/projects/known_fail_canary: a
/// defect that nothing in CI observes is how this subsystem regressed for
/// years (§7's opening premise).
///
/// DO NOT "just switch the gate on" without reading the cryoconfig. That was
/// tried on 2026-08-04: all three binding doors were shut and the compiler
/// and stdlib still self-built clean, but it had to be reverted because the
/// same-module exemption reads the AMBIENT CURSOR, which is wrong often
/// enough to reject calls that never leave their own file. It is blocked on
/// the §5.2 keystone, not on someone noticing.
///
/// ========================================================================
![config(testing)]
namespace ResolutionTripwire::TripwireTest;

import std::core::result;
import std::test::error;
import std::test::assert;

import ResolutionTripwire::Vault;

// ---- KNOWN DEFECT: cross-module `private` call (§3.3, §8.1c) -----------
//
// §3.3: "enforcement is a gate". Today `resolve_qualified_scoped`'s
// single-candidate fast path returns Unique without consulting
// `is_candidate_public`, so a `private` declaration is reachable from
// anywhere as long as it is unambiguous.
//
// §8.1c adds the part that makes this hard: a FUNCTION call is bound by
// sema/call_resolver.cryo, a separate subsystem that never reaches the
// visibility gate at all -- and it has three independent doors.
//
// AFTER THE FIX this must be an E0353 visibility error.

![test]
function WRONG_a_private_function_is_callable_from_another_module()
        -> Result<(), TestError> {
    // `secret` is declared `private` in ResolutionTripwire::Vault.
    // Qualified path: one of the three doors.
    expect_eq(ResolutionTripwire::Vault::secret(), 99)?;
    return Result::Ok(());
}

/// CONTROL, and CORRECT behavior -- must stay green through the fix. If this
/// ever fails alongside the tripwire above, the visibility work broke public
/// access rather than closing the private hole.
![test]
function CONTROL_a_public_function_is_callable_from_another_module()
        -> Result<(), TestError> {
    expect_eq(ResolutionTripwire::Vault::door(), 1)?;
    return Result::Ok(());
}

// ---- KNOWN DEFECT: a bare name resolves with NOTHING in scope (§5.1) ----
//
// §5.1: "The first segment resolves in scope. There is no substring
// matching, no suffix matching, and no dependence on what other modules
// happen to exist in the program."
//
// `ResolutionTripwire::Orphan` has no `import` statement at all, and names
// `Crate` in two signatures. `Crate` is declared only by
// `ResolutionTripwire::Depot`. Nothing binds it in Orphan's scope, so under
// §5.1 both signatures must be an E0203 "unknown type" -- and instead they
// resolve, because cascade step 5 (the GLOBAL LEAF INDEX, types/arena.cryo)
// answers from the whole program without consulting scope at all.
//
// MEASURED 2026-08-05, by deleting the equivalent import from
// tests/projects/generic_name_collision and diffing CRYO_LEAF_AUDIT:
// removing one import moved exactly 6 resolutions onto the leaf index --
// 3 logged with a home module set, 3 logged with an EMPTY use-site, the
// scope-less sites the §5.2 keystone exists to fix. Nothing else in the
// counter moved.
//
// This is §8.2a's root cause reduced to a project small enough to reason
// about: previously it was only observable as an aggregate over a full
// compiler build. It is the reason the leaf index cannot simply be deleted
// -- 2,538 answers in the compiler's own build depend on it, and every one
// of them is a name that was never in scope.
//
// AFTER THE FIX both tests below must be E0203 at the Orphan signatures.
// Convert this project to `compile_fail` with `expect.diagnostic` E0203 in
// the same change, exactly as the visibility tripwire above directs.

![test]
function WRONG_a_static_template_resolves_a_type_not_in_its_scope()
        -> Result<(), TestError> {
    mut c: ResolutionTripwire::Depot::Crate<i64> =
        ResolutionTripwire::Orphan::Packer::pack(21);
    expect_eq(c.unwrap(), 21 as i64)?;
    return Result::Ok(());
}

![test]
function WRONG_a_free_generic_resolves_a_type_not_in_its_scope()
        -> Result<(), TestError> {
    mut c: ResolutionTripwire::Depot::Crate<i64> =
        ResolutionTripwire::Orphan::box_up(23);
    expect_eq(c.unwrap(), 23 as i64)?;
    return Result::Ok(());
}

/// CONTROL. `Depot` is imported nowhere by `Orphan`, but a module that DOES
/// spell the path in full must keep working -- otherwise a fix that made the
/// tripwires above fail did so by breaking qualified paths rather than by
/// enforcing scope.
![test]
function CONTROL_a_qualified_path_still_reaches_the_same_type()
        -> Result<(), TestError> {
    mut c: ResolutionTripwire::Depot::Crate<i64> =
        ResolutionTripwire::Depot::Crate<i64> { v: 25 };
    expect_eq(c.unwrap(), 25 as i64)?;
    return Result::Ok(());
}
